+The domain-bypass attacks can be mitigated through microarchitectural techniques
+such as not allowing faulting loads to provide data to dependent instructions.
+The cross-domain attacks can be mitigated by tagging predictors with information
+such as the privilege mode that identifies the domain in which the prediction was
+created and not using predictions learned in one domain to predict outcomes in
+another domain. The in-domain attacks primarily bypass software-based access
+controls, and these boundaries are typically not visible to hardware.
 
